{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CfLscLuK1BvJY6gPV8TNH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The value is `2` (default), not `1`. The own property is being shadowed by the class field default. The issue: TypeScript class fields use `Object.defineProperty` in the constructor, which overwrites the own property set before upgrade. I need to capture the own property *before* the class field initializer runs — which means in the constructor, before `super()` returns... but that's impossible.\n\nThe standard pattern: capture in constructor before field init. But TS class fields run after super(). The fix is to not use class field syntax for these — use declarations and set defaults in constructor after reconciliation.\n\nWait, actually the issue is simpler. The class fields `emojis`, `minChars`, `maxResults` are initialized with defaults in the class body. When upgrade happens, the constructor runs and those fields overwrite any own properties. The reconciliation in `connectedCallback` happens after the constructor, but by then the class field has already overwritten the own property with the default.\n\nThe fix: move defaults to constructor and do reconciliation there, before setting defaults."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_read_input_tokens":68474,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":454},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d80d6f4-7ee2-4a0c-a7bb-cc79d9229a55","uuid":"111d1455-b517-4069-9b5a-a8f03e5afef5","timestamp":"2026-09-23T18:50:32.329Z","request_id":"req_011CfLscLVWQ4jk76LmZ4E8K"}
